WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.480
So our dad is a documentary film maker

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.580
And when we were growing up he worked on movies about everything

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:08.860
from water in Florida, to education in Alaska.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:13.440
And I guess there is a case to be made that we followed in his footsteps by creating non-fiction video.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.520
Except I don't really think of like this as a documentary.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.960
This is more, you know, like very short podcasts featuring facial expressions.

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:26.400
Which is a genre of non-fiction video that is far less expensive and time consuming than documentaries

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:32.140
and also requires less talent for visual storytelling, which is great because even after more than ten years,

00:00:32.140 --> 00:00:34.580
I'm still pretty bad at editing video.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.240
But one thing I did inherit from Dad was a really deep love for documentary films.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.860
Like, Hank, as you know, my dream job has always been...you know what...

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:46.980
I'm about to date myself, but there used to be this widely adopted technology called cable television.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.500
Basically you would pay a monthly fee in exchange for access to lots of video content.

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.000
So it was sort of like Netflix, except for some reason you still had to watch advertisements..

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.560
It was a weird time...but anyways,

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:59.960
there were these cable stations that just showed old movies all day!

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.280
Like one of them was called "Turner Classic Movies,"

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.600
And before these movies started, there would be this person who would introduce the movie to you

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.440
and talk about some of the historical context and who the stars were.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.620
Now it occurs to me in retrospect that the introducer probably existed mostly to make the movie longer...

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.900
like, if you can stretch a 90 minute movie into 120 minutes of content, then you could add 8 minutes of advertising.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.440
But when I was a kid that was my dream job, to be an introducer.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.220
But not regular movies, of documentaries.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.340
These days, documentaries are a lot easier to discover than they were when I was a kid.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:31.280
Netflix, for instance, has an excellent and extensive library, but

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.270
still, many of my favorite documentaries

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:34.950
aren't on Netflix. Like I'm thinking of

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.540
the "7UP" series, where a filmmaker has

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:37.799
checked in every seven years with a

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:39.390
group of people since they were kids in

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.939
the 1960s. Or "Herb &amp; Dorothy," a movie about

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.920
a postal worker and a librarian living

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.760
in New York City who became two of the world's leading art collectors.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.840
For the classic, "Complaints of a dutiful Daughter,"

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.759
a gut-wrenching and sometimes hilarious

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:52.740
movie about a woman whose mother has Alzheimer's.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.659
Or "Slavery by Another Name,"

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:55.860
a movie about the failures of

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.719
reconstruction and the post-civil war

00:01:57.719 --> 00:02:00.090
United States. There are so many great

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:02.340
documentaries and so few of them ever

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.439
find a broad audience. But as a kid, my

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:06.119
favorite genre of documentary was the

00:02:06.119 --> 00:02:07.799
nature documentary, where you can see

00:02:07.799 --> 00:02:09.899
animals in the wild like fighting to

00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:11.849
survive. I love nature docs because they

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:13.360
felt like a glimpse into a world

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.800
without us, I mean they almost never

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:16.540
include humans in the frame and there's

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.190
something magical about that. But of

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:19.990
course in real life humans can't be

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.550
separated from nature, I mean we're

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.200
currently nature's most important

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.820
species, and the choices we're making

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.160
affect every other species on Earth. And

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.480
that's what I love so much about the

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.160
movie Kedi and why I wanted us to help

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:32.320
in its distribution. Kedi uses all the

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:33.940
conventions of the nature documentary -

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:35.590
you get the beautiful cinematography, the

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.900
fight scenes, the plucky underdog is

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.940
struggling to survive, the parents caring

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.100
for babies, but it doesn't exclude humans

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.870
from the story.  Instead we see the

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.850
connecting points between a city and its

00:02:45.850 --> 00:02:48.070
people and its cats. It is a nature

00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:49.720
documentary that acknowledges that

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.300
humans are part of nature. And it shows

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:54.670
that life for cats is sometimes tender

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.830
and sometimes cold and always

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:59.410
unpredictable, as it is for humans. Now I

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.550
know that Kedi is currently only

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:02.470
available via purchase or YouTube red

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:04.360
and that makes it inaccessible to a lot

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.680
of you. One of the big challenges with

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.510
documentaries is that it's really hard

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:09.160
to fund them on advertising revenue

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:10.989
alone, which is why so few of them are

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:13.060
available on free streaming services, at

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.860
least legally. But regardless, if you do

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.750
have a chance to see it someday, I really

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.760
hope you do. It's a wonderful movie. While

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.080
I'm fulfilling my dream of being an

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:21.940
introducer, two other recommendations: if

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.830
you live in the United States, PBS has

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:25.480
some excellent documentaries that are

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:27.310
actually available for free. Also,

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:28.989
wherever you live, there is a free and

00:03:28.989 --> 00:03:30.670
ongoing documentary project that you

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.180
might enjoy called "The road to Nerdfighteria"

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:34.030
about how different people

00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:35.720
found this little corner of the internet

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.450
and what it means to them. They're still

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.720
accepting submissions and it's a really

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.660
wonderful series, links in the doobly-doo below.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.780
Hank. I will see you on Friday.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:45.130
End Screen! Hank, I actually won't see you

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:46.680
on Friday, that was misleading and I

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:48.360
apologize, but I'm going to be taking

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.049
like a week or possibly two weeks away

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:51.730
from the internet to focus on some

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:53.140
writing things. But there will be a video

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.440
next Tuesday, it's just that I recorded it in advance.

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.280
Okay, check out Kedi and The Road to Nerdfighteria! Bye!

